PLACES=[
N('louisville-city-hall','Louisville City Hall','Louisville','ky','civic','standing',38.25465,-85.76053,'exact','view','walk','','1870–1873',
  'Louisville\'s first building made for city government, raised from 1870 to 1873 to plans by John Andrewartha, on ground that had been the town\'s civic centre since a log courthouse went up across the street in 1784. The mansard clock tower is a replacement, designed by Henry Whitestone after the first one burned in 1875. An annex by Cornelius Curtin followed in 1909, and an 1891 firehouse next door was joined to the group in 1937. Since the city and county merged it houses the Louisville Metro Council.',
  'Three storeys over a raised basement in Indiana limestone, mixing the Italianate and Second Empire styles, under a four-faced clock tower about 195 feet high. Look for the pediment over the main door, carved with the city seal and a steam train under the word "Progress, 1871", and for the carved livestock heads over the side windows. Wikipedia says the inside has been remodelled several times; it was not checked.',
  'A train steaming across the pediment: a city hall that put the railroad on its front door.',
  'A working government building at Sixth and Jefferson streets: look from the sidewalks and from Jefferson Square across Sixth Street. Whether and when the public may go in was not checked.'+DT,
  [W('Louisville City Hall'),['Kentucky Historical Society: Louisville City Hall marker','https://history.ky.gov/markers/louisville-city-hall']],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('louisville-trust-building','Louisville Trust Building','Louisville','ky','civic','standing',38.25528,-85.75861,'site','view','walk','','1889–1891',
  'A seven-storey bank and office block on the southwest corner of Fifth and Market streets, built from 1889 to 1891 to designs by Mason Maury and William J. Dodd. Its 1977 Register form calls it the largest and finest Chicago-style high-rise office building left in Louisville, with a look drawn from H. H. Richardson and the early Louis Sullivan. When the form was written the county owned it and used it for police headquarters and offices; what it holds now was not checked.',
  'A heavy masonry block on concrete footings: a ground floor of Barre, Vermont granite and upper storeys of huge rough-faced blocks of Ohio sandstone, with a square corner tower rising a storey above the cornice and an octagonal turret on top of that. It stands in the old courthouse block, across Court Place from the Jefferson County Courthouse. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from the Fifth Street and Market Street sidewalks. Present owner and use not checked; no source read says the public may go in.'+DT,
  [['National Register nomination form: Louisville Trust Building (1977)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/77000624_text']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours=''),
N('cathedral-of-the-assumption-louisville','Cathedral of the Assumption','Louisville','ky','worship','standing',38.25211,-85.75878,'exact','view','walk','','1849–1852',
  'The mother church of the Archdiocese of Louisville. Its cornerstone was laid in 1849, the year Bishop Flaget decided the old church was too small, and it was dedicated on October 3, 1852; the spire was rebuilt in 1858 after a fire. In the Ohio River flood of 1937 it sheltered people driven from their homes. A long restoration from 1988 found the building cracked down the middle and at risk of collapse; it was rededicated in 1994 and the spire and bell tower were done in 1998.',
  'Inside (from Wikipedia): a blue ceiling set with about 8,000 gold-leaf stars, a fresco of the Assumption found under paint in 1994 and uncovered, sixteen nave windows of 1912, and in the sanctuary the Coronation Window made in 1883 by the Blum Art Company of Louisville, said to be one of the oldest and largest stained glass windows made in the United States. The undercroft was reopened in 1991 after a century unused. When the doors are open was not checked.',
  'A cathedral that was found to be splitting in two, and saved.',
  'An active cathedral at 433 South Fifth Street. Service and visiting times were not read: the cathedral\'s and the archdiocese\'s own pages refused this reader.'+DT,
  [W('Cathedral of the Assumption (Louisville, Kentucky)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('seelbach-hotel-louisville','Seelbach Hotel','Louisville','ky','hotel','standing',38.25085,-85.75785,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1903–1907',
  'A ten-storey French Renaissance hotel at Fourth Street and Muhammad Ali Boulevard (once Walnut Street), built by Louis and Otto Seelbach, brothers from Bavaria who had begun with a bar and grill in 1874. Ground was broken in December 1903; it opened on May 1, 1905, in time for the Derby, and a second part opened on January 1, 1907, bringing it to 500 rooms. F. Scott Fitzgerald stayed there and drew on it for a hotel in The Great Gatsby. After years of decline it was restored from 1979 and reopened on April 12, 1982; it is now a Hilton.',
  'The two lowest floors are faced with stone and the upper eight with brick. Inside, the Rathskeller in the basement is now used as a ballroom. Interior not checked by a visit.',
  'The hotel Fitzgerald borrowed for Daisy\'s wedding.',
  'A working hotel (Seelbach Hilton) at 500 South Fourth Street; it is open as a hotel, and which rooms a non-guest may see was not checked.'+DT,
  [W('Seelbach Hotel')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('brown-hotel-louisville','Brown Hotel','Louisville','ky','hotel','standing',38.24647,-85.75797,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1923',
  'A sixteen-storey hotel at Fourth Street and Broadway, built for the Louisville businessman James Graham Brown to rival the Seelbach a few blocks north. Designed by Preston J. Bradshaw, it opened in 1923 only ten months after work began, at a cost of $4 million. Its chef Fred K. Schmidt invented the Hot Brown sandwich there in 1926. It closed in 1971, two years after Brown died, served as school offices, and was later restored and reopened as a hotel; walls taken down between rooms cut it from 600 rooms to 294.',
  'The tall corner block still runs the hotel it was built for; the two-storey Bluegrass Room was split into one room with three meeting rooms above it during the 1990s restoration. Interior not checked by a visit.',
  'Ten months from ground to guests in 1923, and the Hot Brown to show for it.',
  'A working hotel at 335 West Broadway; it is open as a hotel, and which rooms a non-guest may see was not checked.'+DT,
  [W('Brown Hotel')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('louisville-palace-theatre','Louisville Palace (Loew\'s Theatre)','Louisville','ky','civic','standing',38.24797,-85.75790,'exact','open','walk','','1928',
  'A movie palace on Fourth Street between Broadway and Chestnut, designed by John Eberson in his "atmospheric" manner and opened as Loew\'s Theatre on September 1, 1928, with 3,273 seats, then the largest cinema in Kentucky. It closed for part of the 1970s, reopened in 1981 as a live venue, and was listed on the National Register in 1982. It is now a 2,711-seat concert hall owned by Live Nation.',
  'Inside (from Wikipedia): a Spanish Baroque lobby in deep red and cobalt blue with terrazzo floors, a vaulted ceiling bearing 139 carved faces of composers, writers and philosophers, and an auditorium ringed with building fronts under a painted night sky. The inside is seen by going to a show.',
  'Sit under a fake night sky with Beethoven and Socrates looking down.',
  'A working concert venue at 625 South Fourth Street, open for ticketed shows; no tour or box office hours were read.'+DT,
  [W('Palace Theatre (Louisville, Kentucky)'),['Louisville Palace (shows)','https://www.louisvillepalace.com/']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('louisville-main-library','Louisville Free Public Library, Main Library','Louisville','ky','civic','standing',38.24447,-85.75786,'exact','open','walk','','1905–1908',
  'The Carnegie-funded main library at Fourth and York streets, designed in the Beaux-Arts style by Pilcher and Tachau of New York and finished in 1906; strikes held back its opening to readers until 1908. Its basement once held a natural history museum, and the library kept an armoury with Daniel Boone\'s rifle. A modern North Building was added in 1969. Flood water damaged it badly in 2009, and a renovation in the 2020s opened its old closed stacks to the public.',
  'The 1906 building (the South Building) is entered through the old Delivery Room, now the foyer: two storeys high in the Louis XVI style, with a stained-glass barrel-vaulted ceiling, white marble columns and murals. Opening hours were not read.',
  '',
  'A public library at 301 York Street, open to all at set hours; the library\'s own page refused this reader, so the hours were not checked.'+DT,
  [W('Louisville Main Library')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('gene-snyder-courthouse-louisville','Gene Snyder United States Courthouse','Louisville','ky','civic','standing',38.24703,-85.76247,'exact','view','walk','','1931–1932',
  'A federal post office, courthouse and custom house filling a whole block on Broadway, built in 1931 and 1932 and modelled on the Treasury Building in Washington. The sixth floor was damaged by fire in 1958. The post office moved out in 1986, and from 1986 to 1990 the General Services Administration modernised the offices and put the main lobbies and courtrooms back as they were in 1932.',
  'Six storeys of concrete and steel faced in Bedford limestone, with eighteen fluted Corinthian columns along the Broadway front over a base of arched windows; the fifth floor hides behind the entablature. Inside (from Wikipedia): a long arcaded lobby made for post office patrons, two original courtrooms with coffered wood ceilings and marble wainscots, and a law library with fluted wood pilasters. Not checked by a visit.',
  '',
  'A working federal courthouse at 601 West Broadway: look from Broadway. Federal courthouses screen visitors at the door; whether the public lobbies may be seen without business there was not checked.'+DT,
  [W('Gene Snyder United States Courthouse')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('louisville-gardens-armory','Louisville Gardens (Jefferson County Armory)','Louisville','ky','civic','standing',38.25180,-85.76034,'exact','view','walk','','1905',
  'Built as the Jefferson County Armory and opened in 1905, the hall became the city\'s big indoor arena: it held the SEC men\'s basketball tournament from 1941 to 1952 and concerts into the 1970s, and was renamed Louisville Gardens in 1975. Louisville Public Media reported in June 2025 that it had stood empty since 2008, used at times for city storage, and that the city had picked developers to turn it into film soundstages called Louisville Studios; asbestos clearance had begun.',
  'The outside of an armory-arena of about 6,000 seats on Muhammad Ali Boulevard. The building is being reworked inside, so expect construction fencing. Interior closed.',
  '',
  'Closed while it is redeveloped: view from the sidewalks of Muhammad Ali Boulevard and Sixth Street.'+DT,
  [W('Louisville Gardens'),['Louisville Public Media: Film industry breathes new life into old Louisville Gardens (June 6, 2025)','https://lpm.org/news/2025-06-06/film-industry-breathes-new-life-into-old-louisville-gardens']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours=''),
N('union-station-louisville','Union Station','Louisville','ky','rail','standing',38.24676,-85.76887,'exact','view','walk','stations','1880–1891',
  'The Louisville and Nashville Railroad\'s passenger station on Broadway, begun in 1880 and, after costs rose, finished in 1889 to 1891; it opened formally on September 7, 1891. Henry C. Wolters is credited with the design, with F. W. Mowbray hired for its Richardsonian Romanesque look. At its busiest, in the 1920s, it served 58 trains a day for the L&N, the Monon, the Pennsylvania and others; Amtrak used it until 1976. The city bus agency, TARC, bought it, restored it in 1979 and 1980, and has used it as offices since.',
  'Rough limestone walls from Bowling Green, Kentucky, trimmed in Bedford, Indiana stone, under a slate roof, with a clock tower, smaller towers and turrets. After a fire on July 17, 1905, the rose-coloured windows over the waiting room were replaced by a barrel-vaulted skylight of 84 stained glass panels. Interior not checked.',
  'Fifty-eight trains a day once; now the bus company\'s head office.',
  'Offices of the Transit Authority of River City at 1000 West Broadway: look from Broadway. Whether visitors may see the waiting room was not checked.',
  [W('Union Station (Louisville, Kentucky)')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('st-boniface-church-louisville','St. Boniface Church','Louisville','ky','worship','standing',38.25116,-85.74320,'site','open','walk','','1899–1900 (parish 1836)',
  'Founded in 1836 for German Catholics and named for the Apostle of Germany, St. Boniface is the oldest Catholic parish in Louisville still under its first name. Franciscan friars ran it from 1849 to 1998. The present Gothic church was designed by D. X. Murphy and Brothers, who also designed the friary beside it (1899); Wikipedia says the church was built in 1899, the state marker that it was dedicated in 1900. The friary became apartments in 1991, and the complex is on the National Register as the St. Bonifacius Kirche Complex.',
  'A Gothic church with its friary and the 1908 parish school on East Liberty Street. Interior not checked; the parish lists Sunday Masses, the times to see it.',
  '',
  'An active parish at 531 East Liberty Street; Sunday Mass times from the parish\'s own page. Street parking was not checked.',
  [W("St. Boniface's Catholic Church (Louisville, Kentucky)"),['Kentucky Historical Society: St. Boniface Parish marker','https://history.ky.gov/markers/st-boniface-parish'],['St. Boniface parish','https://www.stbonifaceparish.com/']],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday Mass 8:30 and 11 a.m.'),
N('st-joseph-church-butchertown','St. Joseph Church, Butchertown','Louisville','ky','worship','standing',38.25764,-85.72637,'exact','view','walk','','1886 (steeples 1905–1906)',
  'Bishop Martin J. Spalding founded the parish in 1866 for the German and Irish Catholics of Butchertown. The present church was dedicated in September 1886, and its twin steeples, said to be the tallest in Louisville, were added in 1905 and 1906; a Lourdes grotto followed in 1922. Franciscan friars ran the parish from 1875 to 2002, and its school, taught by Ursuline Sisters until 1975, closed in 2003.',
  'A church with twin steeples on East Washington Street, and a Lourdes grotto of 1922. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active parish at 1406 East Washington Street. Mass times were not read: the archdiocese\'s parish page refused this reader. Street parking was not checked.',
  [['Kentucky Historical Society: St. Joseph Catholic Church marker','https://history.ky.gov/markers/st-joseph-catholic-church']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('louisville-water-tower','Louisville Water Tower and Pumping Station','Louisville','ky','water','standing',38.28041,-85.70204,'site','open','drive','','1860',
  'The first works of the Louisville Water Company, formed in 1854 after cholera and fires had made the case for clean piped water. Theodore Scowden, with his assistant Charles Hermany, dressed the pumping station as a Roman temple and its standpipe as a 183-foot Doric column rising from a Corinthian portico, on a rise by the Ohio River; it began pumping on October 16, 1860. The tornado of March 27, 1890, wrecked the tower, which was rebuilt with a cast-iron pipe, and broke eight of the ten statues on its portico. Pumping moved to Crescent Hill and stopped here in 1909. Tower and station are a National Historic Landmark.',
  'The column and portico, and the original pumping station, whose west wing holds the WaterWorks Museum (opened 2014): old photographs, films, a giant steam engine, and a guided walk into the tower and round the grounds, 30 to 45 minutes.',
  'Wikipedia calls it the oldest ornamental water tower in the world.',
  'Owned by Louisville Water. Entrance at Zorn Avenue and River Road (3005 River Road), with a driveway onto the grounds. Public open days are the second Sunday of each month, guided tours on the hour; tickets at the door by card only ($5 from age 6, less for seniors and military), and every adult must bring a driver\'s license or state ID to be scanned. Group tours by arrangement. Hours from Louisville Water\'s visit page.',
  [W('Louisville Water Tower'),['Louisville Water: visit the Water Tower','https://louisvillewater.com/louisville-water-tower/visit-us/'],['Louisville Water: WaterWorks Museum','https://www.louisvillewater.com/water-tower-park/waterworks-museum']],
  brick='?',hours='Second Sunday of each month 12 to 5 p.m.; tours on the hour from 1 p.m., last at 4 p.m.'),
N('churchill-downs-twin-spires','Churchill Downs and the Twin Spires','Louisville','ky','amuse','standing',38.20417,-85.77178,'site','open','drive','amusements','1895',
  'The racetrack of the Kentucky Derby. New owners built the grandstand with its twin spires in 1895, designed by the Louisville firm of D. X. Murphy, and in 1896 shortened the Derby to a mile and a quarter and began the garland of roses. The spires have been refurbished, but new luxury suites now block a full view of them from most sides; a tornado that damaged the stables left the spires and clubhouse unharmed. The Kentucky Derby Museum is on the grounds.',
  'The two spires over the 1895 grandstand, the symbol of the track and the Derby, seen best from the museum and on race days. The Kentucky Derby Museum, beside the track, covers the race and the breeding and training of thoroughbreds, and runs track tours.',
  'Two spires that stand for a horse race.',
  'The track is private and open on race days with tickets. The Kentucky Derby Museum (704 Central Avenue) is open daily except Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve, Christmas Day, January 4 to 6 and April 29 to May 1, with free parking for visitors in the shared lot with Churchill Downs; hours from the museum\'s visit guide.',
  [W('Churchill Downs'),['Kentucky Derby Museum: visit guide','https://www.derbymuseum.org/visit-guide']],
  brick='?',hours='Museum: March to November, Monday to Saturday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.; December to February, Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('cherokee-park-louisville','Cherokee Park','Louisville','ky','inst','standing',38.24120,-85.69690,'area','public','walk','','1891',
  'A 409-acre city park laid out in 1891 by Frederick Law Olmsted, one of eighteen Louisville parks he designed, along Beargrass Creek, which many foot and road bridges cross. The tornado of April 3, 1974, felled thousands of its old trees; the replanting, paid for largely with federal disaster money, had to follow the park\'s pre-storm Olmsted design, and 2,500 trees and 4,600 shrubs went in. It is cared for with the Louisville Olmsted Parks Conservancy.',
  'Rolling meadows and woods on a 2.4-mile Scenic Loop with a one-way drive and a separate lane for walkers and cyclists; bridges over Beargrass Creek; Hogan\'s Fountain and its pavilion; Big Rock, a large boulder in the creek with a picnic area named for it.',
  '',
  'A public park of Louisville Metro. Parking lots inside the park, one off the Beargrass Creek Trail near the Lexington Road side; park hours were not checked.',
  [W('Cherokee Park')],
  brick='no',hours='',th=[38.24459,-85.69877],thn='Parking lot by the Beargrass Creek Trail, Cherokee Park'),
]

